<Base title="About" description="Product engineering, product leadership and customer experience. How I approach building software, leading delivery and deciding what to build.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      I'm Chiko Mukwenha, a product engineer and product leader from Zimbabwe.
      I work across the decisions that shape a product and the engineering needed
      to make it real. I enjoy getting into the details, but I also want to know why
      we're building something, who it's for and how we'll know it's doing its job.
    </p>

    <p>
      I taught myself to code over a decade ago, starting with Drupal
      and working my way through whatever the problem in front of me demanded.
      That path took me from Harare hackathons to building products
      for organisations in London to shipping my own things in public. At
      <a href="/writing/2019-07-20-leaving-awesome-onesheep/">OneSheep</a>,
      I grew into architecting and leading projects. Later, at
      <a href="/writing/2021-09-18-change/">Customer.io</a>, I worked on a product
      used by millions of people, with a closer connection between the software
      I was contributing to and what happened when customers used it.
    </p>

    <p>
      I care about making things well, and that includes being deliberate about
      what gets built in the first place. Product direction, scope and technical
      decisions all affect what a team can deliver and what it will cost to maintain.
      I like to make those tradeoffs clear, break the work into pieces we can check,
      and stay involved through implementation. Testing, understanding the
      fundamentals and asking why still matter, even when AI helps us move faster.
    </p>

    <p>
      Currently, I'm building <a href="https://usehydra.co.za" target="_blank">Hydra</a>,
      focused on helping South African businesses bring customers back.
      That work brings together customer conversations, product decisions and
      hands-on engineering, including rewards, WhatsApp joining and the tools
      merchants need to run things. It's a useful reminder that a product has to
      make sense in somebody else's working day, however good the implementation is.
    </p>

    <p>
      I write and speak on product engineering and customer experience. This blog
      is where I work through those ideas: how we decide what to build, how we
      deliver it well, and what we learn from the people using it. There are also
      older essays about work, learning and the occasional experiment I run on myself.
      For a more current snapshot, there's my <a href="/now/">now page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Work with me</h2>
    <p>
      If you need a product engineer to build alongside your team, or a head of
      product to help set direction and oversee delivery, I'd be happy to talk.
      I can help work through what to build, make the technical tradeoffs and
      carry those decisions through to working software.
      <a href="mailto:hi@chikomukwenha.co">Tell me about your product</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For talks and conversations on product engineering or customer experience,
      <a href="mailto:hi@chikomukwenha.co?subject=Speaking%20enquiry">get in touch about speaking</a>.
    </p>

